+#### Host API 1.1 filesystem extensions
+
+All filesystem requests carry `providerId` and, for connection-backed providers,
+`connectionId`. This is the filesystem contribution ID, not the connection
+contribution ID. DBX validates the saved plugin/provider/type binding and current
+read-only policy before dispatch. The backend must also validate its own session
+binding and constrain paths to the configured remote root.
+
+| Method | Additional request fields | Result |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `filesystem/stat` | `uri` | Plain entry, not `{ entry }` |
+| `filesystem/copy` | `sourceUri`, `targetUri`, `overwrite` | Mutation result |
+| `filesystem/capabilities` | None | `{ capabilities: [...], ...details }` |
+
+Runtime capabilities narrow the manifest ceiling; they cannot grant undeclared
+operations. Read-only connections further remove mutations, including upload.
+An old backend returning JSON-RPC `-32601` for the capabilities method uses its
+manifest ceiling. Other failures do not trigger a permissive fallback. Unknown
+capability names are rejected. Optional details such as `nativeCopy`,
+`nativeRename`, `copyMode`, `renameMode`, `atomicRename`, and `atomicNoClobber`
+describe backend behavior; they do not grant permissions. Do not claim atomic
+rename or no-clobber guarantees for a check-then-write or copy-delete fallback.
+
+#### Desktop native transfer tasks
+
+These are trusted desktop-host operations, not HTTP or sandbox bridge methods.
+The host-owned UI obtains an absolute local path from the native open/save dialog.
+The backend performs bounded streaming IO directly; file bytes never travel in
+the control RPC. This does not replace binary channels for other plugin domains.
+
+| Method | Additional request fields | Result |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `filesystem/transfer/startUpload` | `uri`, `localPath`, `overwrite` | Full task snapshot |
+| `filesystem/transfer/startDownload` | `uri`, `localPath`, `overwrite` | Full task snapshot |
+| `filesystem/transfer/status` | `transferId` | Task snapshot |
+| `filesystem/transfer/cancel` | `transferId` | Task snapshot |
+| `filesystem/transfer/list` | None | `{ transfers: [...] }` |
+
+Snapshots include `transferId`, `providerId`, `connectionId`, `direction`
+(`upload` or `download`), remote `uri`, `state`, `bytesTransferred`, optional
+`totalBytes`, and optional `error`. States are `queued`, `running`, `completed`,
+`failed`, and `cancelled`. Unknown totals and absent errors may be null or omitted.
+Responses must not contain `localPath`. IDs belong to their connection/provider
+binding, and status/cancel/list must not expose another binding's tasks.
+
+With `host.events`, publish snapshots as `filesystem/transfer/progress`
+notifications. Events are advisory: the host reconciles them through scoped
+status/list requests because events can arrive late or be dropped. Bytes must
+be monotonic and must not exceed a known total. A cancellation request is not
+proof of cancellation; report the terminal state only after IO and cleanup finish.
