+unsafe symlinks and non-file targets, preserve existing destinations unless
+overwrite was explicitly confirmed, and clean up temporary files on failure.
+Native sidecars still execute with the user's OS privileges; these checks are
+product safeguards, not a sandbox for malicious native code.
+
+#### Structured failures
+
+The typed host path preserves JSON-RPC `{ code, message, data? }`, including
+`data.code` (for example `already_exists`, `read_only`, or `partial_success`) and
+opaque `data.recovery`. Recovery may be an object, not just text. The desktop
+command rejects with this object; HTTP returns it as a JSON error body. UI code
+must not reduce it to `String(error)` before deciding whether replacement or
+recovery is appropriate. A partial success must not be retried as a fresh
+destructive operation without reconciling the remote state.
+
+Sandbox response messages retain the legacy `error` string and carry an optional
+`rpcError` object alongside it. The injected SDK rejects with an `Error` whose
+`message`, `code`, and `data` preserve that object. Legacy string-only failures
+remain ordinary `Error` instances.
+
+Connection lifecycle, initialization, and local transfer requests cannot be sent
+through generic sandbox invoke/notify. Remote filesystem operations must pass
+the saved-connection gateway even when invoked through a generic entrypoint.
 
